The '''Constitutional Amendment on Gender Identity Protections''' ({{lang|ca|Esmena Constitucional sobre les Proteccions per Identitat de Gènere}}), colloquially known as the '''Fifteenth Guarantee''', is a {{w|constitutional amendment}} to the [[Twelve Guarantees]] of [[Balboa]] passed by the [[National Assembly of Balboa|National Assembly]] in 2019. The amendment added gender identity to the list of protected categories under the Guarantees' anti-discrimination framework, completing the constitutional protections for LGBT individuals in Balboa.

== Background ==

The [[Constitutional Amendment on Sexual Orientation Protections]] of 2008 added sexual orientation to the protected categories under the [[Twelve Guarantees]], but did not address gender identity. {{w|Transgender}} individuals in Balboa faced legal and practical barriers that were not covered by the existing constitutional framework.

Prior to 2017, legal gender recognition in Balboa required a court-ordered gender recognition certificate, which in turn required medical and psychological evaluation. The 2013 case ''[[Reyes v. Ministry of Health]]'' exposed the circular dependency created by this framework: a medical diagnosis was required for legal recognition, and legal recognition was required for access to {{w|gender-affirming healthcare}}. The [[National Anti-Discrimination Tribunal]] ruled in 2014 that the linkage between healthcare access and the medicalised legal recognition process created a discriminatory barrier, but the ruling did not immediately change the law.

The Gender Recognition Act, passed in 2017, established a {{w|self-determination}} model for legal gender change, allowing individuals to change their legal gender by {{w|statutory declaration}} without requiring medical diagnosis, psychological evaluation, or surgical intervention. The Act also provided for {{w|non-binary}} legal recognition. Despite this legislative progress, the absence of constitutional protections for gender identity meant that {{w|discrimination}} against transgender individuals could only be challenged under the general {{w|anti-discrimination law}} framework, which did not explicitly enumerate gender identity.

== Campaign ==

The campaign for the amendment was led by transgender rights organisations and broader LGBT advocacy groups. [[Adriana Reyes i Soler]], the plaintiff in the ''Reyes'' case, became a prominent advocate for the amendment following the 2014 ruling. The campaign emphasised that the [[Constitutional Amendment on Sex-Based Discrimination]] (1986) and the [[Constitutional Amendment on Sexual Orientation Protections]] (2008) had established the precedent for expanding the Guarantees, and that gender identity required the same level of constitutional protection.

The 2016 case ''[[Sòtol Community Coalition v. Tramuntana Provincial Government]]'' demonstrated the continuing need for constitutional protections. The case, which addressed the exclusion of LGBT organisations from provincial funding and the absence of LGBT content in education curricula, highlighted the vulnerability of transgender individuals to discrimination in areas where constitutional protections were not in place.

Opposition was led by conservative and religious groups, who argued that the amendment would compromise women's protections and create conflicts with sex-based rights. Proponents countered that the amendment addressed a distinct category of discrimination and did not diminish existing sex-based protections.

== Provisions ==

The amendment added gender identity to the protected categories under the [[Twelve Guarantees]] framework. It prohibits {{w|discrimination}} on the basis of gender identity in employment, housing, education, healthcare, and access to goods and services. Complaints are adjudicated by the [[National Anti-Discrimination Tribunal]], which has the power to order remedies including compensation, reinstatement, and {{w|injunction}}s.

The amendment complements the Gender Recognition Act (2017) by providing constitutional-level protection against discrimination, while the Act provides the statutory framework for legal gender recognition. Together, the two instruments establish that transgender individuals are entitled to both legal recognition of their gender identity and protection from discrimination on that basis.

== Text ==

The amendment reads as follows:

{{Quote|'''Fifteenth Guarantee''' — No person shall be subjected to discrimination on the basis of gender identity in any sphere of public life, including employment, housing, education, healthcare, and the provision of goods and services. The protections afforded under this Guarantee shall apply to all persons regardless of their gender identity or expression, and shall not be construed as diminishing the protections afforded under the Thirteenth Guarantee. The National Anti-Discrimination Tribunal shall have jurisdiction to adjudicate complaints arising under this Guarantee.}}

== Passage ==

The amendment passed with the required {{w|supermajority}} in the National Assembly and was ratified by [[Adonian Community]] review. The [[Balboan People's Party|BPP]] government supported the amendment, with additional support from the [[Liberal Party of Balboa|Liberal Party]], the [[Socialist Front of Balboa]], and the [[Balboa Green Party]].

== Legacy ==

The amendment was the third and most recent expansion of the Twelve Guarantees. It completed the constitutional framework for LGBT rights in Balboa, which includes protections for sexual orientation (2008) and gender identity (2019), statutory rights to civil partnership (2004) and marriage equality (2011), and self-determination-based gender recognition (2017).

Following the amendment's passage, the 2019 Inclusive Education Amendment mandated that all provinces include age-appropriate content on sexual orientation and gender identity in secondary school curricula, addressing the curriculum issue raised in the ''Sòtol Community Coalition'' case.

